      "costUsd": 0.010078499999999999,
      "pronunciationText": "Now turn the counts into probabilities. Divide each count by the number of rolls, two hundred. Thirty-eight out of two hundred is zero point one nine. That fraction of the rolls is called a relative frequency. Now watch the bars. Every bar is divided by the same number, so the shape doesn't change, only the scale. These numbers are estimates, so we write approximately equals, not equals. A relative frequency describes these two hundred rolls. The probability belongs to the die itself. Replay the rolls and watch the estimate for a four. After ten rolls it read zero point four. After fifty, it was already near zero point two. More rolls usually give a better estimate."
