      "outputTokens": 1483,
      "costUsd": 0.013451999999999999,
      "pronunciationText": "Data is one way to get a distribution. A model is the other: reasoning about how the experiment works. If the die is fair, its six faces are equally likely, so each value has probability one sixth, before a single roll. So in a question, if you're given counts, you're estimating from data. If you're told how the experiment works, you're using a model. Now compare. The data gave zero point one nine for a four. The model says one sixth, about zero point one six seven. That's almost five more fours than a fair die would average. Is that a big gap? Here are three runs of two hundred rolls from a simulated fair die. Look how much their bars wobble. In each of these fair runs, some face is further from thirty-three than our four is. So a gap this size happens easily with a fair die. That's an illustration, not a formal test, but these data aren't strong evidence that the die is unfair."
